$$
\begin{aligned}
f(x)&=\frac{x^{2}-4x+4}{x^{1/2}}\\
&=x^{\frac{3}{2}}-4x^{\frac{1}{2}}+4x^{-\frac{1}{2}}
\end{aligned}
$$

Apply the power rule:

$$
f'(x)=\frac{3}{2}x^{\frac{1}{2}}-2x^{-\frac{1}{2}}-2x^{-\frac{3}{2}}
$$

The tangent slope at $\left(4,2\right)$ is

$$
\begin{aligned}
m=f'(4)&=\frac{3}{2}\left(4\right)^{\frac{1}{2}}-2\left(4\right)^{-\frac{1}{2}}-2\left(4\right)^{-\frac{3}{2}}\\
&=3-1-\frac{1}{4}\\
&=\frac{7}{4}
\end{aligned}
$$

The normal is perpendicular to the tangent, so take the negative reciprocal:

$$
\begin{aligned}
m'&=-\frac{1}{m}\\
&=-\frac{1}{\left(\frac{7}{4}\right)}\\
&=-\frac{4}{7}
\end{aligned}
$$

17592186710954.question/worked-solution:
Let $f(x)=\frac{(x+2)^2}{\sqrt{x}}$. Expand the numerator and divide each term by $x^{1/2}$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
f(x)&=\frac{x^{2}+4x+4}{x^{1/2}}\\
&=x^{\frac{3}{2}}+4x^{\frac{1}{2}}+4x^{-\frac{1}{2}}
\end{aligned}
$$

Apply the power rule:

$$
f'(x)=\frac{3}{2}x^{\frac{1}{2}}+2x^{-\frac{1}{2}}-2x^{-\frac{3}{2}}
$$

The tangent slope at $\left(9,\frac{121}{3}\right)$ is

$$
\begin{aligned}
m=f'(9)&=\frac{3}{2}\left(9\right)^{\frac{1}{2}}+2\left(9\right)^{-\frac{1}{2}}-2\left(9\right)^{-\frac{3}{2}}\\
&=\frac{9}{2}+\frac{2}{3}-\frac{2}{27}\\
&=\frac{275}{54}
\end{aligned}
$$

The normal is perpendicular to the tangent, so take the negative reciprocal:

$$
\begin{aligned}
m'&=-\frac{1}{m}\\
&=-\frac{1}{\left(\frac{275}{54}\right)}\\
&=-\frac{54}{275}
\end{aligned}
$$

17592186711017.question/worked-solution:
Rewrite the radical or reciprocal using powers, then differentiate.

$$
\begin{aligned}
f(x)&=-1 + \frac{1}{x}, \\f'(x)&=- \frac{1}{x^{2}}=- \frac{1}{x^{2}}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Evaluate the tangent slope and take its negative reciprocal to find the normal slope.

$$
\begin{aligned}
m_t=f'(2)&=- \frac{1}{(2)^{2}}=- \frac{1}{4}, \\m_n=-\frac{1}{m_t}&=-\frac{1}{\left(- \frac{1}{4}\right)}=4.
\end{aligned}
$$

Use point-slope form through $\left(2,- \frac{1}{2}\right)$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
y-\left(- \frac{1}{2}\right)&=4(x-(2)), \\y&=4 x - \frac{17}{2}.
\end{aligned}
$$

At the $x$-axis, $y=0$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
0&=4x+\left(- \frac{17}{2}\right), \\x&=\frac{17}{8}.
\end{aligned}
$$

The intersection is $\left(\frac{17}{8},0\right)$.

17592186711025.question/worked-solution:
Rewrite the radical or reciprocal using powers, then differentiate.

$$
\begin{aligned}
f(x)&=x^{\frac{3}{2}} - 3, \\f'(x)&=\frac{3 x^{\frac{1}{2}}}{2}=\frac{3 \sqrt{x}}{2}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Evaluate the tangent slope and take its negative reciprocal to find the normal slope.

$$
\begin{aligned}
m_t=f'(4)&=\frac{3 \sqrt{(4)}}{2}=3, \\m_n=-\frac{1}{m_t}&=-\frac{1}{(3)}=- \frac{1}{3}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Use point-slope form through $(4,5)$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
y-(5)&=- \frac{1}{3}(x-(4)), \\y&=- \frac{x}{3} + \frac{19}{3}.
\end{aligned}
$$

At the $y$-axis, $x=0$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
y&=- \frac{1}{3}\cdot 0+\left(\frac{19}{3}\right), \\y&=\frac{19}{3}.
\end{aligned}
$$

The intersection is $\left(0,\frac{19}{3}\right)$.

17592186711030.question/worked-solution:
Rewrite the radical or reciprocal using powers, then differentiate.

$$
\begin{aligned}
f(x)&=- 3 x^{\frac{1}{4}}, \\f'(x)&=- \frac{3}{4 x^{\frac{3}{4}}}=- \frac{3}{4 x^{\frac{3}{4}}}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Evaluate the tangent slope and take its negative reciprocal to find the normal slope.

$$
\begin{aligned}
m_t=f'(16)&=- \frac{3}{4 (16)^{\frac{3}{4}}}=- \frac{3}{32}, \\m_n=-\frac{1}{m_t}&=-\frac{1}{\left(- \frac{3}{32}\right)}=\frac{32}{3}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Use point-slope form through $(16,-6)$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
y-(-6)&=\frac{32}{3}(x-(16)), \\y&=\frac{32 x}{3} - \frac{530}{3}.
\end{aligned}
$$

At the $x$-axis, $y=0$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
0&=\frac{32}{3}x+\left(- \frac{530}{3}\right), \\x&=\frac{265}{16}.
\end{aligned}
$$

The intersection is $\left(\frac{265}{16},0\right)$.

