y-(2)&=- \frac{1}{2}(x-(1)), \\y&=- \frac{x}{2} + \frac{5}{2}.
\end{aligned}
$$

At the $y$-axis, $x=0$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
y&=- \frac{1}{2}\cdot 0+\left(\frac{5}{2}\right), \\y&=\frac{5}{2}.
\end{aligned}
$$

The intersection is $\left(0,\frac{5}{2}\right)$.

17592186711041.question/worked-solution:
Rewrite the radical or reciprocal using powers, then differentiate.

$$
\begin{aligned}
f(x)&=- x^{\frac{1}{2}} - 1, \\f'(x)&=- \frac{1}{2 x^{\frac{1}{2}}}=- \frac{1}{2 \sqrt{x}}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Evaluate the tangent slope and take its negative reciprocal to find the normal slope.

$$
\begin{aligned}
m_t=f'(1)&=- \frac{1}{2 \sqrt{(1)}}=- \frac{1}{2}, \\m_n=-\frac{1}{m_t}&=-\frac{1}{\left(- \frac{1}{2}\right)}=2.
\end{aligned}
$$

Use point-slope form through $(1,-2)$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
y-(-2)&=2(x-(1)), \\y&=2 x - 4.
\end{aligned}
$$

At the $x$-axis, $y=0$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
0&=2x+(-4), \\x&=2.
\end{aligned}
$$

The intersection is $(2,0)$.
